WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.319 --> 00:00:03.220
Film Courage: How does someone know what story
to write?

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:07.700
Evan Kidd, Filmmaker/Podcaster: I think a
lot of times before you write a story you

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:11.940
have to ask yourself why do I need to be the
person to tell it?

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:17.430
And a lot of times you have to say is there
anyone else better qualified than me?

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:23.330
I think sometimes people think I can tell
every story, I can tell any story.

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:24.330
Maybe?

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:29.410
But for me it’s always what am I qualified
to tell?

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:37.850
I prefer to tell stories that have some semblance
of truth in my life or someone I know or something

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:42.440
I’ve seen just because to me they feel a
little more personal.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:47.990
I would certainly take a check from HBO to
write for GAME OF THRONES but I can’t quite

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:53.930
relate to the fantasy character and dragons
and whatnot in the same way that I can relate

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:58.480
to more real and naturalistic things that
happen in my life.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:04.199
And that’s not to say that you can’t incorporate
those things in a fantasy piece or surreal

00:01:04.199 --> 00:01:06.500
piece or anything like that.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:12.760
But just for me I tend to gravitate toward
the kind of more naturalistic real to life

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:18.710
things whether it’s funny stuff, serious
stuff, more experiential based things I’ve

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:19.710
gone through.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:23.999
Film Courage: What advice do you have for
someone who can’t finish writing their story?

00:01:23.999 --> 00:01:26.740
Is that possibly the wrong story for them?

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:29.470
Or it just hasn’t worked itself out yet?

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:34.850
Evan: I think when you write something you’re
always going to hit a roadblock somewhere.

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:39.120
I’d be kind of surprised if you made it
through smooth sailing without hitting any

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.649
kind of roadblock.

00:01:41.649 --> 00:01:46.540
If you’re struggling though I would say
think about why.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:51.140
Is it because you’re not connecting if you’re
not caring about the characters it could be

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:56.530
that because if you don’t care about your
characters the audience definitely won’t

00:01:56.530 --> 00:02:02.049
care about your characters and so I’ve noticed
over the years with writing like I mentioned

00:02:02.049 --> 00:02:06.050
earlier there is a couple of scripts on my
computer that sit unfinished and a lot of

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:08.690
that is because I couldn’t figure it out.

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:11.470
A lot of times it was I lost interest you
know?

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:13.630
It stopped being fun to write.

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:19.540
For me whenever writing isn’t enjoyable
anymore I put it on ice, I pause it and I

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:21.860
say I can’t do this right now.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:24.230
Maybe a year from now I pick it back up and
it’s more interesting.

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:29.360
Maybe I have lived enough life in that year
of time where I am able to see the characters

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:31.380
with new eyes.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:35.630
But a lot of times it could be that the characters
are too much for the writer.

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:41.320
It could be that the writer doesn’t have
the experience or the writer doesn’t need

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:43.190
to be the person telling that story.

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:46.530
Maybe there’s a more qualified writer to
tell it.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:48.340
I think it could certainly vary.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:52.460
Film Courage: What about caring about your
character too much?

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:59.480
Evan: Yes, caring about your character too
much I think is essential though.

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:04.820
I think if you don’t care too much then
maybe you don’t care at all?

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:05.820
I don’t know.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:13.090
I think I’ve never quite felt…and this
is only me speaking…lukewarm or neutral

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:15.690
to any of my characters.

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:21.930
When I write the main character itself I think
I want to go to bat for these characters.

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:23.041
I really want to fight for them.

00:03:23.041 --> 00:03:26.891
I want them to win even if it’s a sad story.

00:03:26.891 --> 00:03:31.490
I want them to find some kind of catharsis
whether they find it in the film or not.

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:36.280
A lot of times I’ll leave a film vague and
I’ll say maybe they do, maybe they don’t.

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:39.310
But I like to at least crack the door a little
bit.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:44.210
So yes, I think caring about your characters
is super crucial.

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:49.490
I would say the only reason to be cautious
of caring too much would be if you feel that

00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:55.150
you’re showing a bias toward that character
at the expense of all your other characters.

00:03:55.150 --> 00:04:00.200
For instance, if you care so much about your
main character that none of the other characters

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:06.320
in your film get to challenge that main character,
get to grow with that main character.

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:12.500
I like to think that main character starts
the film and ends the film a different person

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:15.080
unless there is something you’re going for
that they don’t do that.

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:19.460
But in most films you want to see some sort
of journey for your character.

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:20.220
Question For The Viewers: What are the biggest
reasons why you have stopped writing a screenplay?

